    <h2 class="section-title">Can You Use Both?</h2>
    <div class="service-content-full">
      <p><strong>Yes, strategically.</strong> Here are common scenarios:</p>

      <h3>CNC Now, OIC Later</h3>
      <p>If you're in crisis, get CNC status for immediate relief. Once you stabilize financially, you can apply for an OIC to permanently resolve the debt before the IRS removes your CNC status.</p>

      <h3>OIC Rejected, Then CNC</h3>
      <p>If your OIC is rejected and you can't afford an installment agreement, CNC may be your fallback. The time spent on the OIC process added to your collection statute, so keep that in mind.</p>

      <h3>CNC as a Bridge</h3>
      <p>Some taxpayers use CNC as a temporary measure while they work on improving their finances, then pursue an OIC from a position of stability.</p>
    </div>
